Road, Beruwala. 2. Mohamed Ajward Mohamed Akram Pasha. 83, Gorakaduwa, Maradana, Beruwala. 3. Mohamed Ajward Fathimah 
Makeena 22B, Arab Road, Beruwala. 4. Mohamed Ajward Fathimah Fahmiya 22B, Arab Road, Beruwala. 5. Mohamed Ajward 
Juwairiya 24/8, Saviya Road, Mahagoda, Beruwala. 2 6. Mohamed Ismail Mohamed Marsook 31, Bakir Markar Mawatha, 
Maradana, Beruwala 7. Mohamed Marsook Mohamed Mishkath 31, Bakir Markar Mawatha, Maradana, Beruwala 8. Mohamed Marsook 
Mohamed Minhaj 31 Bakir Markar Mawatha, Maradana, Beruwala 9. Mohamed Marsook Fathimah Masheena 31 Bakir Markar 
Mawatha, Maradana, Beruwala 10. Mohamed Marsook Fathimah Madiha 31 Bakir Markar Mawatha, Maradana, Beruwala 11. Shahul 
Hameed Mohamed Halimdeen No. 72, Chinafort, Beruwala 12. Mohamed Ismail Marikkar Mohamed Mawjood Marikkar Shazuliya 
Road, Chinafort, Beruwala. 13. Mohamed Kamaldeen Mohamed Ajmal Sally, No. 74 Chinafort, Beruwala. 14. Mohamed 
Kamaldeen Mohamed Ashfan No. 74 Chinafort, Beruwala 3 15. Nation Trust Bank PLC No. 242, Union Place, Colombo 02. AND 
In the matter of an application in terms Of Section 18 of the Civil Procedure Code Shahul Hameed Mohamed Halimdeen No. 
72, China Fort, Beruwala 11thDefendant-petitioner Vs Mohamed Ajward Athaullah 50, Wethhimarajapurah, Maradana, 
Beruwela Plaintiff-Respondent AND NOW BETWEEN Shahul Hameed Mohamed Halimdeen No. 72, Chinafort, Beruwala 11th 
Defendant-Petitioner-Petitioner Vs Mohamed Ajward Athaullah 50, Wethhimarajapurah, Maradana, Beruwala. 
Plaintiff-Respondent-Respondent Before : R. Gurusinghe, J. & Dr. S. Premachandra, J. 4 Counsel : M.U.M. Ali Sabry PC, 
with Ruwantha Cooray, Naamiq Nafath, Vimukthi Karunarathne and Akash Raffeek instructed by Ramzi Bacha Associates for 
the 11th Defendant-Petitioner-Petitioner Arguedon : 08-05-2026 Decided on: 30-06-2026 JUDGMENT R. Gurusinghe, J. The 
plaintiff-respondent (The Respondent) instituted Partition Action bearing no. P/8647 in the District Court of Kalutara 
by plaint dated 07-12-2023, seeking the partition of the Land and Premises more fully described in the schedule 
annexed to the plaint. The petitioner was a party to the said partition action as the 11th defendant in the said 
action, on the basis of Deed of Transfer No. 4451 dated 07-02- 2014, attested by G.G. Arulpragasam, Notary Public of 
Colombo, which was produced and marked in the District Court as ‘පැ-33’. The petitioner was further made a party to 
the proceedings on the allegation that the petitioner had unlawfully been constructing a building upon the land sought 
to be partitioned. The respondents obtained an Enjoining Order and a Notice of Injunction against the petitioner on 
11-12-2023. The petitioner then filed objections to the application for injunction. In his objections, the petitioner 
averred that he had sold his interest in the subject matter of the action to Deebro Holdings (Pvt) Limited by Deed of 
Transfer No. 243 dated 06-12-2023, attested by Dhammika Jiminige, Notary Public. The petitioner further stated that he 
did not undertake any construction within the corpus sought to be partitioned, and he no longer possesses any right, 
title, or interest in the corpus at all. On that basis, he moved Court to discharge him from the Partition 
proceedings. The petitioner also stated that he is unable to comply 5 with the said Enjoining Order, as he no longer 
has any legal interests or rights in the corpus of the action. Subsequently, the respondents applied to the District 
Court to add Deebro Holdings (Pvt) Limited as a party defendant to the action and to issue an Enjoining Order and a 
Notice of Injunction against Deebro Holdings (Pvt) Limited. Accordingly, the Learned District Judge of Kalutara 
directed Deebro Holdings Pvt Limited, to be added as the 16th defendant to the action and that an Enjoining Order and 
Notice of Injunction, to be issued against the said 16th defendant. The application of the petitioner seeking to be 
discharged from the Partition Action was refused by the Learned District Judge of Kalutara, by order dated 14-02-2024, 
on the ground that the 11th defendant has mortgaged his rights in the subject property to the 15th defendant in the 
action, namely, Nations Trust Bank. Aggrieved by the said Order, the petitioner filed the present application seeking 
to have the order dated 14-02-2024 set aside, which refused to discharge the petitioner from the proceedings in the 
Partition Action. The petitioner pleaded the following as exceptional grounds warranting the intervention of this 
Court, by exercising restitution jurisdiction. A. The said orders are contrary to law; B. Learned District Judge has 
failed to take cognizance of the fact that the Petitioner has alienated his title in the subject matter prior to the 
institution of the captioned action; C. Learned District Judge has erred in law in applying Section 18 of the Civil 
Procedure Code; D. Learned District Judge has misdirected himself in interpreting Section 5 of the Partition Law No. 
21 of 1977 (as amended); E. Learned District Judge has failed to understand that the Petitioner is not a necessary 
party in terms of Section 5 of the Partition Law No. 21 of 1977 (as amended); F. Learned District Judge has erred in 
law in failing to determine that the Petitioner has no right title and interest in the subject matter; 6 G. Learned 
District Judge has erred in law in failing to take cognizance of the fact that the Respondent has not established a 
prima facie case; H. Learned District Judge has erred in law in failing to take cognizance of the fact that the 
balance of convenience favours the Petitioner; I. Learned District Judge has erred in law in failing to take 
cognizance of the fact that the Respondent is not entitled to interim relief; J. Learned District Judge has erred in 
law in failing to determine that the Respondent having instituted the action against the wrong party, is not entitled 
to subsequently amend the Plaint and/or add a party; K. The impugned Orders of the Learned District Judge are contrary 
to the principles laid down in the case of Don Alwis vs. Village Committee of Hiripitiya 54 NLR 225. The Learned 
District Judge refused the petitioner’s application to be discharged from the Partition Action on the basis that a 
mortgage continued to subsist over the subject property and that the petitioner is required to remain a party to the 
Partition Action. The petitioner produced Deed of Transfer No. 243 dated 06-12-2023 and proved that the petitioner had 
already divested himself entirely of all right, title, and interest in the subject matter before institution of the 
Partition Action. It is pertinent to note that the Partition Action was filed on 07-12- 2023, one day after the 
execution of the said deed of transfer. Necessary parties who are required to be joined to a partition Action are 
described in Section 5 of the Partition Law No. 21 of 1997 (as amended). Section 5 of the Partition Law No. 21 of 1977 
(as amended) is as follows: 5. The plaintiff in a partition action shall include in his plaint as parties to the 
action all persons who, whether in actual possession or not, to his knowledge are entitled or claim to be entitled – 
(a) To any right, share or interest to, of, or in the land to which the action relates, whether vested or contingent, 
and whether by way of mortgage, lease, usufruct, servitude, trust, life interest, or otherwise, or 7 (A) To any 
improvements made or effected on or to the land: Provided that in the case of a mortgage, the mortgagee or any person 
claiming any interest under him shall be included as a party only if he has registered an address for service of legal 
documents in terms of sections 6 and 28 of the Mortgage Act; Provided further that if such mortgagee or person 
aforesaid claims under an instrument executed more than fifteen years prior to the institution of the action, he shall 
not be a necessary party to such action, unless he has registered an address for service of legal documents in terms 
of the aforesaid provisions of the Mortgage Act within a period of ten years prior to the date of institution of the 
action. The petitioner is neither a co-owner of the subject property nor a person presently possessing any. The 
petitioner has no right, share or interest in the corpus of the Partition Action. The material placed before Court 
indicates that the petitioner had divested himself of all right, title, and interest in the property prior to the 
institution of the action. It is clear from the provisions of Section 5 of the Partition Law that the petitioner is 
not a necessary party. The Learned District Judge was of the opinion that, because of the subsisting mortgage over the 
property, the petitioner should remain a party to the proceedings. However, since the petitioner has divested all his 
right, title and interest in the corpus, he cannot be a necessary party. With regard to a mortgage, the plaintiff 
should make the mortgagee or any other person who claims an interest under the mortgage the party to the action, only 
if an address is registered for service of legal documents, in terms of the provisions of Section 5 of the Partition 
Law. In the case of Abeysekera vs Harmanis Appu 14 NLR353 the Supreme Court stated inter alia as follows; Before 
considering these questions, it may be advisable to deal shortly with the history of the procedure by way of 
restitutio in integrum. Under the civil law, where a person suffered a legal prejudice by the operation of law, the 
praetor, having personally inquired into the matter (causaecognitio) in the exercise of his imperium, which enabled 
him to consider all the actual facts of the case, might issue a decree re-establishing the original legal position, 
that is to say; replacing the person injured in his previous condition. In Roman law, 8 restitutio in integrum was the 
removal of a disadvantage in law which had legally occurred. It was a protection against justice (as distinguished 
from an action against injustice), which was rendered necessary on account of the practical impossibility of taking 
legally, in advance, all the circumstances into consideration that in reality may occur. (Sohm's Institutes of Roman 
Law, s. 56, 111, Burge, 2nd ed., vol. 4, chap. 1.) The decision of the Learned District Judge is erroneous and 
inconsistent with the provisions of section 5 of the Partition Law No. 21 of 1997. In consequence of the said 
decision, the petitioner suffered legal prejudice. Therefore, the petitioner is entitled to make this application. The 
respondents have not filed any objection to the petitioner’s application to discharge him from the proceedings. 
Although notices were issued on the respondents on several occasions, in the present application, none of the 
respondents appeared before the Court to oppose or contest the reliefs sought by the petitioner. The discharge of the 
petitioner from the Partition Action will not prejudice the rights of the other parties to the action. In the above 
circumstances, the order of the District Court of Kalutara in case No. P 8647 dated 14-02-2024 is set aside. The 
Learned District Judge of Kalutara is directed to discharge the 11th defendant-petitioner from the partition Action P 
8647. Judge of the Court of Appeal. Dr. S. Premachandra J. I agree. Judge of the Court of Appeal.



