(Formerly 6th Plaintiff) 21A. Eliyadura Kusumawathi Silva All of Managoda, Bandaragama Road, Waskaduwa. 
DEFENDANTS-RESPONDENTS AND NOW Page 6 of 17 Sandaradura Ranjith Wimal De Silva, Managoda, Bandaragama Road, Waskaduwa. 
PETITIONER-PETITIONER Vs. 1. Eliyadura Kusumalatha Silva 2. Eliyadura Elan De Silva (Deceased) 2A. Fransiskudura Nimal 
Chandralal Silva 4. Eliyadura Harrison Silva 5. Eliyadura Disilin Silva 7. Eliyadura Winson Silva 9. Eliyadura Jasalin 
Nona 10. Fransiskudura Saidin De Silva (Deceased) 10A.Fransiskudura Chandani De Silva All of Managoda, Bandaragama 
Road, Waskaduwa. PLAINTIFFS-RESPONDENTS￾RESPONDENTS 1. Agampodi Nanasiri De Silva 2. Agampodi Padmawathi (Deceased) 
2A. Wettamuni Sirisena Silva 3. Janguge Wimalawathi Page 7 of 17 4. Munasinghe Lionel 5. Sandradura Seelawathi Silva 
(Deceased) 5A. Janaka Raveendra Silva 6. Walimuni Alicenona (Deceased) 6A. Muthumuni Namawathi Silva No.116A, Niwdawa, 
Alubomulla. 7. Unnamed 8. Unnamed 9. Ranmuni Eron Silva 10.Munasinghe Nancynona Silva 11.Ranmuni Alicenona 
12.Wettamuni Sirisena Silva 13.Wettamuni Janaki Jayamathi Silva 14.Wettamuni Shantha Kumara De Silva 15.Wettamuni 
Nandana Prema Kumara Silva 16. Wettamuni Pushpa Renuka Jayamanthi Silva 17. Walichchoru Hewage Wilbert Silva 18. 
Munasinghe Ruvilin Silva (Deceased) 18A.Munasinghe Ranjith Silva 19. Eliyadura Saraneris Silva (Formerly 3rd 
Plaintiff) 19A. Eliyadura Pradeep Indika Silva Page 8 of 17 20. Eliyadura Edson Silva (Formerly 8th Plaintiff) 21. 
Eliyadura Milton Silva (Deceased) (Formerly 6th Plaintiff) 21A. Eliyadura Kusumawathi Silva All of Managoda, 
Bandaragama Road, Waskaduwa. DEFENDANTS-RESPONDENTS- RESPONDENTS Before: R. Gurusinghe J. & Dr. Sumudu Premachandra J. 
Counsel: Thilan Liyanage with Shehan De Vas Gunawardena for the Petitioner – Petitioner. Nithil Fernandopulle with 
Asanka Lowe instructed by Dammika Jeminige for the Plaintiff-Respondent￾Respondent. Written Submissions: By the 
Petitioner on 16/06/2026. By the 1st, 2A, 4A, 5A, 7A,9th and 10A Respondents on 16/06/2026. Argued On: 15/05/2026. 
Judgement On: 30/06/2026. Page 9 of 17 Dr. Sumudu Premachandra J. 1] The Petitioner details a long-standing Partition 
Action regarding a plot of land called "Gabadawaththa", initiated in the District Court of Kalutara (DC Kalutara Case 
No.7099/Partition) in February 2002, with a subsequent judgement delivered on 28/11/2008, and the Interlocutory Decree 
entered accordingly. 2] The conflict arose on 29/01/2021, when a Licensed Surveyor attempted to execute a final survey 
of said partition action but allegedly entered the Petitioner's land on a pretext, removing concrete boundary posts. 
The Petitioner and his wife objected because a portion of their land, separately registered under the Registration of 
Title Act No. 21 of 1998 with a First Class Title of Absolute Ownership for lot No. 152, was wrongfully included in 
the Partition Corpus. 3] This resistance prompted Court summons and contempt charges under Section 793 of the Civil 
Procedure Code, initiated by the 9th Plaintiff-Respondent (annexed as F and G, alongside responding documents R1 and 
R2). 4] In response, the Petitioner filed an application under Section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code seeking the 
exclusion of his registered land from the Corpus and requesting a commission to superimpose Plan No. 299 onto the Map 
(supported by annexed documents P1-P8, H, P3, P7, I, and J). 5] On 26/11/2021, the Additional District Judge of 
Kalutara dismissed the Petitioner's Section 839 application, holding Per Incuriam that the Petitioner and his wife 
should be named as accused parties in the contempt matter, and ruling that the application could not be maintained 
under the Registration of Title Act (with the proceedings and Order annexed as K, L, and journal entries as M). Page 
10 of 17 6] The Petitioner seeks Revision or Restitutio in Integrum from the Court, asserting that proceeding with the 
Partition scheme would lead to the irreversible demolition of his residence's western boundary wall and the loss of 
roughly 2 perches of land. 7] Along with the Petition, certified copies of the original Plaint and amended caption 
marked as A and A1, a preliminary survey plan (No. 3434) and report marked as B and B1 (and further detailed as P1, 
P2, and H), alongside the judgement and amended Interlocutory Decree marked as C and D are annexed. Following 
objections to an initial final scheme of Partition (Plan No. 4005), a settlement led to an alternative Partition 
scheme prepared by Licensed Surveyor D. Lasantha Hewagama (Plan No. 299 dated 10/09/2012), which is annexed to the 
Petition as document E. 8] In the above backdrop, the Petitioner prays that this Court be pleased to; a) Issue Notice 
on the Respondents; b) Grant an Interim Order staying further proceedings in the case bearing No. 7099/P in the 
District Court of Kalutara until the final determination of this application; c) Make Order revising and/or setting 
aside the Order dated 26/11/2021 which had been delivered by the learned Additional District Judge of Kalutara in the 
above case; d) Make Order adding the Petitioner as a Defendant to this action for the limited purpose of the 
Petitioner been permitted to take the necessary steps required to be granted an exclusion of the land he is entitled 
to in terms of the Registration of Title Act No.21 of 1998 from the Corpus sought to be partitioned in this case; Page 
11 of 17 e) Make Order permitting the Petitioner to take the necessary steps in the above case to issue a commission 
to identify the extent of his land that had been erroneously included into the Corpus; f) Make Order amending or 
modifying the judgement and the Interlocutory Decree entered in the above case in so far as to exclude the extent of 
the Petitioner’s land which is more fully described in the third schedule of the Petition filed on behalf of the 
Petitioner as prayed for and in terms of his application made under Section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code (Document 
“H”); g) Make Order in this case restoring and/or recognising the legal rights that the Petitioner had been vested 
with in terms of the Registration of Title Act No.21 of 1998; h) Make Order revising and/or setting aside all steps/ 
proceedings/ Orders made and taken with regard to the carrying out of the final survey in the above case; i) Grant 
costs; j) For such other and further relief Your Lordships’ Court shall deem fit. 9] The Respondents deny the claims 
made in the Petitioner's application. They outline numerous preliminary legal objections asserting that the 
application is misconceived in law, represents an abuse of the court process to harass the Respondents, and should be 
dismissed in limine (at the threshold). They argue the Petitioner is guilty of laches (unreasonable delay) and has 
deliberately suppressed and misrepresented material facts from a related Partition case (No. 7099/P) in the District 
Court of Kalutara. Page 12 of 17 10] They show an escalation of this application, where a charge sheet for contempt of 
court was issued against the Petitioner and his wife following an illegal encroachment on an access road. To support 
their arguments, the Respondents submit the evidence, including a Primary Courts Procedure Act Magistrate’s Order 
(marked V1) and a Village Plan (marked V2), which confirms the separate nature of their adjoining lands. 11] The 
Petitioner says that he only discovered the attachment of his land to the Partition action when a Surveyor and Police 
Officer entered his land in January 2021 and attempted to remove concrete posts on his Eastern boundary. He stressed 
that the Certificate of Title that grants him the right to exclude his land from the Partition, an alleged contempt of 
court charge under Section 793 of the Civil Procedure Code, and a separate revision application (Case No. RV 11/2022) 
filed in the High Court of Kalutara challenging a Primary Courts Procedure Act Order. 12] On perusal of the Partition 
action No. 7099/P, instituted by the Original Plaintiffs in the District Court of Kalutara, it is seen that all 
statutory steps under the Partition Law, including the registration of “Lis Pendens” and the preparation of the 
preliminary plan, were duly followed. 13] The Judgement was delivered on 28/11/2008, determining the Corpus and 
shares, against which no Appeal, Revision, or Restitutio-in-Integrum application was preferred against the said 
Decree. However, during the final survey, the Petitioner objected, claiming that the survey encroached upon his land, 
leading to contempt of court proceedings against him. The Petitioner then sought relief in the District Court under 
Section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code to exclude his land, leading to the current Revision and 
Restitutio-in-Integrum application challenging the District Judge's refusal. Page 13 of 17 14] The Petitioner’s 
primary contention is that a Certificate of Title issued under the Registration of Title Act, No. 21 of 1998, takes 
supremacy over the Partition Action and that his land was unlawfully included in the Corpus. 15] This court sees that 
the Petitioner's claims are uncertain, as his own Petition and Affidavit conflict regarding the exact extent of land 
allegedly encroached upon, varying from "approximately 2 perches" to other irreconcilable extents. It is further seen 
that the Petitioner has failed to obtain a commission and prepare a superimposed plan to identify the overlapping 
portions, and it would be a mere assertion that his land, subject to a first-class title, has been encroached upon in 
the Partition action. 16] The Petitioner admits that the impugned Partition action was filed in the year 2000, and his 
first-class Title Certificate was given well after 07/03/2019. (vide I). The preliminary survey was carried out on 
08-09th June 2001. It is seen that, at the preliminary survey, the new claimant, Eron Silva, had made his claim. (Vide 
B1). Further, when the Partition action was filed, his title had not been cleared under Registration of Title Act. 17] 
Moreover, if the Petitioner’s land was encroached, he would have objected at the preliminary survey. Now, after the 
Interlocutory Decree was entered, the Petitioner has stated that approximately two perches were encroached upon. This 
claim was made at the final survey, well after the Interlocutory Decree. Thus, the Petitioner is guilty of laches. 
Page 14 of 17 18] In Sri Lanka Insurance Corporation Ltd. v. Shanmugam and Another, [1995] 1 SLR 55, His Lordship 
Ranaraja J noted the following, where the party slept over his rights as; “The principle on which this court has to 
act is not whether the court that gave judgment was tricked into it, but whether one party to action was deceived by 
the conduct of the opposing party. Clearly it was not the case in this instance. It was entirely due to the lack of 
due diligence on the part of the petitioner that it took no steps to file answer. Thereafter it failed to file the 
necessary papers within the stipulated period to have the decree set aside. Had the petitioner filed its answer at the 
proper time, it could have taken up all the defences which it claims would have deprived the respondents of the 
judgment obtained in their favour. Thus, it cannot now complain of a denial of justice.” [Emphasis is added] 19] It 
should be noted that the Interlocutory Decree was entered in 2011. The effect of Interlocutory Decree is considered in 
Madurapperuma v Wijesundara and Others 2019 (1) SLR 512, which established that a District Court has no Jurisdiction 
to vary its own judgement or restrict a settled Corpus using inherent powers once an Interlocutory Decree has achieved 
finality under Section 48(1) of the Partition Law. His Lordship SAMAYAWARDHENA, J. noted; “Section 48(1) of the 
Partition Law invests Interlocutory and Final Decrees of partition with finality notwithstanding any omission or 
defect of procedure (subject to sub-sections (4) and (5) of section 48; and any decision on any appeal which may be 
preferred).” Page 15 of 17 20] The Petitioner is not a party to the Partition action. In Perera vs Wijewickrama 15 NLR 
411, Pereira J. held that; “the remedy of restitutio in integrum can only be availed of by one who is actually a party 
to the contract or legal proceeding in respect of which restitution is desired.” 21] Thus, the Petitioner cannot 
directly dispute the final scheme of Partition. In Menchinahamy vs Munaweera, 52 NLR 409, held as follows; “The remedy 
by way of restitutio in integrum is an extraordinary remedy and is given only under very exceptional circumstances. It 
is only a party to a contract or to legal proceedings who can ask for this relief. The remedy must be sought for with 
the utmost promptitude. It is not available if the applicant has any other remedy open to him” 22] As noted above, the 
Petitioner ought to have made the claim at the preliminary survey; thus, this remedy is not available for him. 23] The 
documents show that the District Court of Kalutara action is filed to Partition a land called “Gabadawaththa” 
(Document "A"). After a judgement and an amended Interlocutory Decree were delivered in November 2008 (Documents "C" 
and "D") and an alternative Partition scheme was settled in September 2012 (Document "E"). 24] I now consider the 
statutory protection afforded to the Petitioner under the Registration of Title Act, No. 21 of 1998. The Petitioner 
holds a Certificate of Title granting him a First Class Title of Absolute Ownership over Lot No. 152 on Map Page 16 of 
17 No. 530164, issued by the Surveyor General and declared by the Commissioner of Title Settlement. Under Section 
32(1) of the Act, such registration vests absolute ownership of the land parcel along with all rights and privileges 
free from all unexpressed interests. Furthermore, Section 33(1) and 33(2) dictate that entries in the Title Register 
serve as conclusive evidence of ownership that cannot be questioned or assailed in a court of law except as provided 
directly within the Act. 25] However, the Petitioner’s land was not subjected to Partition. It was a boundary dispute 
between the Petitioner’s land and the land sought to be Partitioned. Thus, the issue of the title cannot be 
considered. Though, the Petitioner emphasizes the supremacy of the Registration of Title Act over the Partition Act by 
citing explicit statutory provisions, it has no direct impact when the issue of boundaries is concerned. 26] Under 
Section 63 of the Registration of Title Act, No. 21 of 1998, the Partition Law does not apply to land parcels 
registered with a First Class Title of Absolute Ownership or a Second Class Title. If a land is already governed by 
the Act, Partition actions cannot be filed to exclude or partition it. The section says; “63. The Partition Act shall 
not apply to land parcels registered under this Act with a First Class Title of Absolute Ownership, or Second Class 
Title of Ownership.” 27] This is to say that any disputes regarding boundaries, rights, or exclusion of lands that 
hold a Title Certificate must be resolved through the Commissioner of Title Settlement, rather than through a 
Partition lawsuit in the District Court. If any party dissatisfied the decision of the Commissioner of Title 
Settlement, that party can appeal to the pertinent District Court.(See section 22 and 29 of Registration of Title Act, 
No. 21 of 1998) 28] In the instant application, when the Partition Decree was entered in 2011, the Petitioner’s title 
was in dispute; that is, no Title Certificate was granted. Thus, section 63 of Registration of Title Act, No. 21 of 
1998 has no application Page 17 of 17 to this as the issue is to be decided at the date of institution of the 
Partition case. (Vide Talagune v De Livera, [1997] 1 Sri LR 253). Moreover, the dispute is not for title of each land, 
it is for boundaries. Thus, supremacy under the Title Registration Act is immaterial. 29] It is to be noted that in 
Title Registration Act cases, the District Court is vested with Appellate Jurisdiction. The Title Registration Act 
provides a scheme for dispute resolution. Thus, the Petitioner has an alternative remedy. On the other hand, the issue 
of a conflict between the two Acts does not arise due to this factual uncertainty, and the Petitioner must suffer the 
consequences of his own folly. Further, this court is of the view that the inherent powers under Section 839 of the 
Civil Procedure Code cannot be widely invoked to displace the strict statutory framework of the Partition Law. 30] The 
impugned Partition subject matter and the Petitioner’s land are two distinct, adjacent lots; no title dispute is 
involved. Granting relief is a discretionary remedy of this court. Considering the finality of the Partition Decree, 
the guilt of the Petitioner to claim the land at the preliminary survey and laches for 11 years, for not filing this 
suit, we decline to exercise the discretion. 31] Therefore, the application is dismissed. No costs. JUDGE OF THE COURT 
OF APPEAL R. GURUSINGHE J. I agree JUDGE OF THE COURT OF APPEAL



