it on insufficient or inaccurate information and there is abundant authority that it has that power." The reason for 
doing so is that want of due registration of lis pendens has the same effect as failure to register the lis pendens at 
all and renders the issue of summons and all further proceedings null and void. Even an interlocutory decree entered 
under such circumstances would be null and void. If all the proceedings from and after the registration of lis pendens 
are null and void, the resulting position would be that the action is still pending, and any person who has not been 
made a party will have the right to intervene and to be added as party. In an unreported case, S.C. 74-B.C. (Inty.) 
